:: warrior_queen_day_3_1

/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
   DAY 3 — THE RAPIER INTRODUCTION
   Part 1: Morning — The Corset's First Dawn
═══════════════════════════════════════════════ */

I woke before the summons.

Not from discipline — from //discomfort//. The breasts had spent the night growing, apparently, because the dull ache that had been a background hum when I fell asleep was now a proper throb, insistent and warm, pulsing in both mounds with the rhythm of a second heartbeat. The tissue felt //tighter//. Fuller. As though someone had, during the night, gently inflated two small cushions beneath my skin.

I lay still, staring at the ceiling, and assessed the situation with the clinical detachment I was learning to cultivate. A detachment that lasted approximately four seconds before my hand drifted to my chest.

The left breast was... //more//. More than yesterday. Not dramatically — not the sudden leap of the growth-dream that had plagued my sleep on the first night — but measurably. The mound was rounder beneath my fingertips. The tissue denser. The inframammary fold — that crease Veletia had measured, the line where breast met ribcage — was deeper, more defined. Yesterday, my fingers had smoothed over a gentle transition from chest wall to breast curve. Today, there was a //ledge//. A small one, barely perceptible, but present: the point where the breast began to hang rather than merely sit.

I cupped the breast in my palm. The flesh filled my hand differently than it had two days ago. There was more of it. The weight was slightly greater — a few grams, perhaps, nothing a scale would catch but something my hand, which had been touching this chest for twenty years and only recently been introduced to its new tenants, could not miss.

The nipple was harder this morning. Not from cold — the chamber was warm — but from some internal process, some growth-related engorgement that kept the nub raised and prominent against the centre of the areola. I could feel it pressing against my palm: a small, stiff point, radiating a low, constant signal that said //I am here, I am new, I am bigger than I was yesterday and I will be bigger still tomorrow//.

<<speech "mc">>Good morning to you too,<</speech>> I told the breast.

It did not respond. But it continued throbbing, which I chose to interpret as acknowledgment.

[[Put on the training corset|warrior_queen_day_3_2]]


:: warrior_queen_day_3_2

The training corset was waiting on the chair where I had draped it last night. I picked it up and held it for a moment, examining it in the dawn light. Grey linen. Steel boning. Lambskin-lined cups. Nipple padding. A garment designed for a woman's body, built for combat.

//My// garment. For //my// body.

I stripped the sleeping shift. The cool air found the breasts immediately — the nipples puckered, the areolae tightening, goosebumps spreading across the pale skin of the new tissue in that now-familiar cascade of involuntary response. I watched it happen in the mirror — watched my own nipples harden, watched the goosebumps chase each other across the small mounds — and felt the peculiar dissonance of observing a process that was entirely automatic, entirely female, and entirely mine.

The salve first. I opened the jar, scooped a small amount onto my fingertips, and rubbed them together to warm the cream. Moonpetal scent filled the air — sweet, silvery, alive with the faint tingle of transformation alchemy.

I applied it to the left breast. Circular motions, outer to inner, as Maera had shown me. The skin was smooth beneath my fingers, the tissue warm and yielding, and the sensation of touching my own breast with deliberate, attentive care was becoming — incrementally, reluctantly — less alien. Not normal. But //familiar//.

The areola. The sensitivity sharpened as my fingers crossed the border — the ring of darker skin was a threshold between general awareness and specific, acute attention. I circled the areola gently, pressing the salve into the Montgomery glands — those tiny raised bumps that Veletia had counted, twelve on the left, ten on the right — and each bump responded to the pressure with a small, bright signal that ran inward toward the nipple like tributaries feeding a river.

The nipple. I touched it last, briefly, a single pass of salve-slicked fingertips across the stiff nub. The sensation //bloomed// — that radiating pulse, deep and warm, spreading from the nipple through the breast and down into the chest. Not pleasure. Not pain. Something the body was still learning to categorize, filing under //new// and //intense// and //pay attention//.

Right breast. Same process. Slightly less sensitive, slightly smaller — the asymmetry was subtle but real, and Veletia had assured me it was entirely normal. "Every woman in the history of the world has had one breast slightly larger than the other," she had said. "It is one of femininity's less-advertised features."

Salve applied. Breasts glistening.

I reached for the training corset and put it on.

The cups received the breasts with the precise, cradling hold that had made me gasp yesterday. The lambskin lining — warm from the chamber air, impossibly soft — settled against the salve-slicked skin and the contact was //soothing//. The nipples, cushioned by the double padding, softened from their hard morning erection into a gentler, more relaxed state. The boning engaged the core. The lacing tightened at the back.

Supported. Held. Accounted for.

I pulled on the training leathers over the corset. The chest piece sat better — the corset's smooth exterior provided a layer between skin and leather, and the shaped cups meant the breast tissue was not being directly compressed by the flat breastplate. The nipple impressions that had horrified me on Day 1 were invisible — the padding smoothed the contour into a gentle, ambiguous curve that could have been muscle or could have been breast and offered certainty to neither.

I examined myself in the mirror. The figure looking back was a warrior. Not the prince — not the broad, hard-edged prince with the flat chest and the swaggering shoulders. But a warrior nonetheless: leathers fitted, corset beneath, rapier belt... wait. I didn't have the rapier belt yet.

But I would. Today, if Aldric's "bring nothing" instruction meant what I thought it meant, today I would hold a new weapon.

I braided my hair — a single thick braid down the back, the way I had seen Yseldra wear hers. The hair was long enough now, well past the shoulders, and braiding it kept it from falling across my face during training. The act of braiding — fingers weaving, pulling, the satisfying tug of hair being organized — was oddly calming. A morning ritual that the prince had never possessed and the person I was becoming was slowly adopting.

[[Head to the Iron Garden|warrior_queen_day_3_3]]


:: warrior_queen_day_3_3

The corridor was quiet. Dawn's light bled through the arrow slits in pale, cold bars. My boots — slightly loose now, the feet having narrowed by a fraction — echoed on the flagstones.

I passed the entrance to the Great Library and nearly collided with Jeral.

He emerged from the library door with an armful of scrolls, his spectacles perched crookedly on his thin nose, his muted blue tunic ink-stained at the cuffs. He saw me, stopped, and performed the rapid visual assessment that I was learning to recognize: the eyes tracking from face to chest to hips to feet, cataloguing changes with the efficiency of a man whose entire purpose was to observe, record, and file.

<<speech "jeral">>Good morning, Your Highness. Training again?<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>As always.<</speech>>

<<speech "jeral">>If I may — the leather at the shoulder seems to be sitting differently today. The fit has improved. Is that Mistress Veletia's doing?<</speech>>

Of course Jeral noticed. Jeral noticed //everything//. He noticed which servants arrived late. He noticed which councillors had been drinking. He noticed the angle of the Queen's eyebrow during petitions and adjusted his recommendations accordingly. He was a human instrument of observation, and nothing escaped his lens.

<<speech "mc">>She made some adjustments, yes.<</speech>>

<<speech "jeral">>Wise. The previous arrangement was... conspicuous. In ways that invited comment from those who lack the discretion to remain silent.<</speech>>

He said it carefully. Diplomatically. The way Jeral said everything — with a precision that conveyed exactly as much information as he intended and not one syllable more. But beneath the diplomacy, the meaning was clear: //I saw the soldiers laughing at you. I heard them. And I want you to know that I noticed, and that I judge them for it, not you.//

<<speech "mc">>Thank you, Jeral.<</speech>>

He adjusted his spectacles — a nervous habit, or perhaps a tactical one; Jeral's habits were difficult to distinguish from his strategies.

<<speech "jeral">>Your Highness. For what it is worth — the changes are becoming... increasingly evident. I say this not to discomfort you, but because it is my duty to inform you of things you may not wish to hear. The court is talking. The servants are talking. And the soldiers —<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>The soldiers will think what they think.<</speech>>

<<speech "jeral">>The soldiers will think what Sir Aldric //permits// them to think, which is not the same thing at all.<</speech>> A ghost of a smile crossed his thin face. <<speech "jeral">>If I may offer an observation from the administrative perspective: the more visibly capable you become in training, the less the physical changes will be the subject of gossip and the more they will be the subject of... awe. People mock what confuses them. They revere what impresses them. Give them something to be impressed by, and the confusion will become irrelevant.<</speech>>

He shifted the scrolls in his arms, executed a precise half-bow, and continued on his way.

I stood in the corridor and thought about what he had said. //Give them something to be impressed by.// The rapier, perhaps. Or whatever Aldric had planned for today.

I walked to the Iron Garden with Jeral's words in my skull and the training corset holding my breasts and the braid swinging against my back like a pendulum marking time.

[[Enter the Iron Garden — Day 3|warrior_queen_day_3_4]]


:: warrior_queen_day_3_4

Aldric was there. The mist was there. The cobblestones were there. The universe's commitment to consistency in the Iron Garden was, as ever, absolute.

But today, something was different.

Aldric held no practice blade. Frostfang was at his hip, but sheathed. Instead, in his hands, he held something slender — a weapon I had seen in the armoury a hundred times and never once considered carrying.

A Velmorian court rapier.

Slender as a reed. Light as a willow switch. The blade no wider than two fingers pressed together. The pommel was worked silver, worn smooth by generations of hands. The guard was an elegant swept shell — designed, I would later learn, not merely for aesthetics but to catch and trap an opponent's blade. The whole weapon weighed perhaps half what my longsword did.

It looked like something a lord would hang above a fireplace to impress dinner guests. Not a weapon. A decoration with pretensions.

He extended it toward me, hilt-first.

<<speech "aldric">>Your new blade.<</speech>>

I took it. Turned it in my hands. The balance was... unexpected. The longsword had been heavy, front-weighted, designed to generate force through its own mass. The rapier was balanced at the guard — the weight sitting in the hand rather than ahead of it, making the blade feel like an extension of the wrist rather than a burden on the arm.

I could not keep the contempt from my voice.

<<speech "mc">>You expect me to fight with this? It is a needle, Aldric. Not a sword.<</speech>>

Aldric said nothing.

He drew Frostfang.

[[Aldric attacks|warrior_queen_day_3_5]]


:: warrior_queen_day_3_5

The greatsword cleared the scabbard with a sound like tearing silk.

Before I could register what was happening — before my brain could bridge the gap between "training conversation" and "I am about to die" — he attacked. Not a drill. Not a measured demonstration. A full-speed, full-force assault — Frostfang sweeping in a lateral arc aimed at my midsection with enough power to cleave a warhorse in two.

Instinct saved me. Twelve years of training under this man had welded certain reflexes into my nervous system so deeply that they operated beneath thought, beneath choice, in the raw animal territory of //move or die//.

I threw the rapier up. A desperate parry. The blades met with a sound that vibrated through my wrist, up my arm, into my teeth, and through my collarbone. The rapier held — the steel was finer than it looked, tempered to flex without shattering — but the impact drove me backward three staggering steps, each step jolting through the training corset, through the breasts, through the core that was still burning from yesterday's stance work.

Aldric advanced. Frostfang rose for an overhead cleave — the killing stroke, the one that would have split me from crown to collarbone if the blade were not blunted. I twisted aside. The greatsword //cratered// the cobblestone where I had been standing, sending chips of rock skittering across the yard like frightened insects.

He reversed into a backhand cut. I ducked — felt the wind of the blade part my hair, my longer, finer hair that whipped across my face as I dove, strands catching on my lips and eyelashes — and stumbled backward until my spine struck the training yard wall.

Aldric stopped.

Frostfang's point hovered one inch from my throat. I could feel its cold exhalation on my skin. The promise of steel that did not bluff.

The silence lasted three heartbeats. My heartbeats — hammering so fiercely I could feel them in the budding flesh of my chest, each beat pressing the breast tissue outward against the corset's cups in a pulse-pulse-pulse of terrified life.

<<speech "aldric">>Light does not mean weak.<</speech>>

His voice was quiet. Controlled. The voice of a man who had just attacked at killing speed and was no more winded than if he had sneezed.

<<speech "aldric">>It means //fast//. And fast kills slow. Every time.<</speech>>

He sheathed Frostfang. The sound of steel entering leather was, in that moment, the most beautiful noise in the world.

I remained pressed against the wall. The rapier in my grip, white-knuckled. My chest heaving — and I was //aware// of the heaving in a way I had never been before, because the chest that was heaving contained breasts, and the breasts were moving with each laboured breath, rising and falling against the corset's cups, and the motion was visible through the tunic, and I could //feel// it — the tissue expanding and contracting, the corset holding it steady, the nipples pressing against the padding with each inhalation.

The proximity of death had made every part of this changing body more real. More //present//. As though the body had decided, in the moment of potential annihilation, to remind me of everything it was becoming. //You are alive,// the breasts said through their aching. //You are changing. And you are still here.//.

I pushed off the wall. My legs were shaking. My hands were shaking. The rapier was shaking.

But I was alive. And the rapier was in my hand.

[[Learning the guards|warrior_queen_day_3_6]]


:: warrior_queen_day_3_6

<<speech "aldric">>Three guards. Three positions. Learn them and the rapier becomes a language. Fail to learn them and the rapier becomes a very expensive stick.<</speech>>

He produced a second rapier — a practice blade, blunted — and demonstrated the first guard.

<<speech "aldric">>Prima. The first guard.<</speech>>

He raised the rapier high — blade angled forward, point aimed at an invisible opponent's face. His body turned slightly, presenting a narrow profile. And his off-hand — the left, the hand without a weapon — rose behind him, fingers spread, palm facing upward.

<<speech "aldric">>The off-hand is not decoration. It is counterweight. When the blade is high, the body wants to tip forward — especially now, with the chest weight. The off-hand behind corrects the balance. Like a dancer's arm.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>A dancer's arm.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Fencing is dancing with the intention to kill. The body mechanics are the same. Assume prima.<</speech>>

I raised the rapier. Point forward. Body turned.

And here — //here// — the revelation began.

The old sword stance had required me to face the opponent squarely. Shoulders forward. Chest presented. A broad target that relied on armour and mass to absorb strikes. The rapier's prima guard turned me //sideways// — presenting the narrowest possible profile to the enemy.

The narrowest possible profile of a body with breasts was... interesting.

Turned sideways, the chest was almost invisible. The corset's cups, which created a visible curve from the front, presented only a thin edge from the side. My narrowing shoulders helped — less width to present. The overall target I offered the enemy was a sliver of person: one shoulder, one hip, one leg. A body that was becoming smaller, narrower, lighter — a body that the longsword had treated as a liability — was suddenly, in this stance, an //advantage//.

<<speech "aldric">>Seconda. The second guard.<</speech>>

He swept the blade left, body turning further upon the hips — using the hip rotation to power the blade's arc. The off-hand tucked in.

<<speech "aldric">>This guard uses the hip rotation. The wider the hips, the stronger the arc. You have wider hips than you did a month ago. Use them.<</speech>>

I swept the rapier left. The hips — my wider, broader, stubbornly expanding hips — drove the rotation, and the blade //whipped// through the air with a speed and force that startled me. The rotation was //bigger// than it had been. Aldric's lesson from yesterday echoed: a man's hips generate ninety degrees. A woman's generate a hundred and ten. That extra twenty degrees was not merely theoretical — I could //feel// it, the additional arc, the extended range, the blade reaching further than the old body could have sent it.

<<speech "aldric">>You felt that.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>...Yes.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Good. Remember it. That is what your hips are //for//. They are not an inconvenience. They are a weapon. Terza. Third guard.<</speech>>

[[Terza — the low guard|warrior_queen_day_3_7]]


:: warrior_queen_day_3_7

<<speech "aldric">>Terza. Low guard. The predator's crouch.<</speech>>

He sank. Knees bending, weight dropping into the hips — the feminine guard's territory, the deep centre I had been drilling for two days — and the rapier descended with him, point angled upward from the low position like a scorpion's tail, coiled and patient and lethal.

<<speech "aldric">>This guard uses everything the feminine stance teaches. Low centre. Hip stability. The blade comes from below — from an angle the enemy does not expect, because men are trained to watch for strikes from above and the sides. A blade that rises from the ground is a blade they do not see until it is already inside their guard.<</speech>>

I assumed terza. Sank into the feminine guard. Let the hips take the weight, let the core engage, let the rapier's point angle upward.

And //something happened//.

The rapier responded to this body.

It was the clearest, most undeniable revelation I had experienced since the transformation began — clearer than the first time I felt the breasts grow, clearer than the moment the feminine guard clicked, clearer than anything. The rapier //fit//.

The longsword had been forged for the prince. For broad shoulders that could brace behind a two-handed grip. For thick wrists that could absorb impact. For a flat chest that presented no obstacle to the blade's path. Every technique assumed mass. Assumed power. Assumed the wielder could muscle through resistance the way a bull muscles through a fence.

The rapier asked for //none of those things//.

It wanted finesse. It craved //wrist articulation// — and my wrists, thinner now, more flexible, the tendons reshaping themselves beneath Thalara's patient alchemy, could manipulate the blade with a precision my old hands had never achieved. The old thick wrists had been clubs. These new, narrowing wrists were //instruments//. They could rotate the blade through angles that the old joints had physically not permitted — a subtle supination, a flick of the ulna, a wrist-roll that sent the rapier's point tracing spirals in the air.

It wanted the off-hand free for balance — and my shifting centre of gravity made that counterweight not merely useful but //essential//. The old body, top-heavy, had not needed a counterbalance. This body — lower-centred, chest-weighted, hip-anchored — //required// the off-hand's presence to maintain equilibrium during lunges and recoveries.

It wanted a narrow profile — and the body I was growing was //becoming// narrow. Shoulders shrinking. Waist tapering. The sideways presentation of the rapier stance reduced me to a sliver of target that was, with each week of transformation, becoming slimmer still.

For the first time since the potions had begun their work — for the first time in weeks of grief and confusion and the slow dissolution of the body I had known — I held a weapon that did not feel as though it was fighting my body.

The rapier moved where I directed it. The thrusts were clean. The parries crisp. When I lunged from the feminine guard, the rapier extended like a natural limb — like a finger pointing at death — and for one bright, burning moment I felt not like a broken swordsman relearning his alphabet, but like something //new//.

Something that might, given time and sweat and the stubborn refusal to yield, become lethal.

<<speech "mc">>It... fits.<</speech>>

Aldric watched me with those grey eyes.

<<speech "aldric">>Every weapon has a body it was made for. The longsword was made for the body you are leaving. The rapier was made for the body you are building. The weapon did not change. //You// did.<</speech>>

I held the rapier in terza and breathed. The blade hummed in my hand — alive with potential, alive with the kinship between a weapon and the body it was designed to serve.

This was mine. This blade, this stance, this body.

For the first time, all three agreed.

[[Drilling the lunge sequence|warrior_queen_day_3_8]]


:: warrior_queen_day_3_8

The morning dissolved into drills.

Lunge. Recover. Lunge. Recover. The rapier's basic vocabulary — a conversation between blade and body, repeated until the words became instinct.

The lunge in the feminine guard was different from the longsword's. The old lunge had been a forward explosion — rear leg driving, front leg reaching, the entire body mass thrown behind the blade like a battering ram. Powerful. Linear. And, as Aldric had demonstrated on Day 1, entirely dependent on shoulders and thighs that I no longer possessed in their original configuration.

The rapier lunge was driven from the //hips//. The rear hip snapped forward, rotating the torso, and the blade rode the rotation like a surfer riding a wave — arriving at the target with speed that was generated not by mass but by angular momentum. The wider the hips, the more powerful the snap. The lower the centre, the more stable the platform.

I lunged. And lunged. And lunged.

The motion was percussive — each lunge jolting through the body from toe to fingertip. And each jolt found the breasts.

The training corset held them better than anything I had worn before. The cups contained the tissue, the boning stabilized the torso, the lambskin lining cushioned the nipples. But no garment, however brilliantly engineered, could entirely eliminate the //physics// of breast tissue during explosive movement. The breasts had mass. Mass that obeyed Newton's laws with the same cheerful indifference as any other mass. When the body lunged forward and stopped, the breasts wanted to //continue// — and the corset caught them, absorbed the momentum, and pressed them back. But the motion was there. A small, internal surge. A pulse of weight shifting inside the cups. A sensation that said, with each lunge: //we are here, we are moving, we are physics.//.

I was learning to incorporate it. Not fight it — //use// it. The breast surge during the lunge was a timing signal. When I felt the tissue press forward against the cups, I was at full extension. When I felt it settle back, I was recovered. The body was teaching me its own rhythms, and the rapier was learning to speak the language.

By the twentieth lunge, the motion was smooth. By the fiftieth, it was fast. By the hundredth, the rapier's point was arriving at the target — a mark chalked on the training dummy's chest — within a palm's breadth every time.

Aldric watched. Circled. Corrected my wrist angle once — a quarter-turn inward — and my elbow position twice. He did not comment on the breasts. He did not comment on the corset. He treated the body as he always had: as a system to be optimized. If the system now included breasts, then the system would be optimized around breasts. Simple. Mechanical. Aldric.

The heat rose. The sun crested the eastern wall. The tunic clung to sweat-dampened skin, and the corset beneath grew warm and damp against the breast tissue, and the world narrowed to the rapier and the lunge and the endless, repetitive, meditative //thrust-recover-thrust-recover// that was carving new pathways through my muscles and my mind.

[[Midday — the bouncing|warrior_queen_day_3_9]]


:: warrior_queen_day_3_9

The humiliation arrived at midday.

It always arrived at midday. The morning was for learning. Midday was for being reminded that learning occurred inside a body that the world had opinions about.

I was drilling a lunge sequence at speed — thrust, recover, thrust, recover — building rhythm and velocity, pushing the body toward the limit where control frayed and instinct took over. The motion was percussive, driven from the hips, and I was //good// at it — the rapier's point hitting the target zone consistently, the recovery clean, the stance solid.

But I had made a mistake.

The corset's lacing had loosened. Sweat, heat, and three hours of explosive movement had worked the leather ties fractionally free, and the cups — which had held the breast tissue in firm, controlled containment all morning — now had //play//. A centimetre of slack. Perhaps less.

It was enough.

The lunge sequence set my budding breasts //bouncing//.

Not wildly. Not dramatically. They were still small — barely filling the loosened cups — and the corset still provided structure. But the combination of loosened support and percussive hip-driven movement produced a visible oscillation. Each lunge sent the tissue pressing forward against the cups. Each recovery let it drop back. The result, beneath the thin training tunic that was clinging to the sweat-soaked corset with the shameless transparency of wet linen, was an unmistakable, rhythmic //bounce//.

I did not notice. I was inside the drill, inside the rhythm, inside the trance-state that repetitive physical practice induces. The body was moving. The blade was striking. The world was small and focused and concerned with nothing except the next thrust.

Two soldiers on the rampart above the yard noticed.

I heard the snicker first.

[[The snicker|warrior_queen_day_3_10]]


:: warrior_queen_day_3_10

A muffled, mean little sound. The laughter of men who knew they were being cruel and savoured it — the specific, poisonous amusement of people watching someone struggle with a vulnerability and finding it //funny//.

Then a whispered remark. I could not make out the words, but I understood the tone with the perfect clarity of a person who has heard that tone before and knows exactly what it means. Something about tits. Something about the prince who was growing a pair. Something that reduced twenty years of swordsmanship and three days of desperate, aching, brave reinvention to a //joke//.

Shame hit me like a lance through the ribs.

My face blazed. The heat climbed from my collar to my hairline in a wave that felt like sunburn from the inside. My rhythm shattered. The rapier dipped. The lunge died mid-extension, the point drooping toward the cobblestones, and I stood in the centre of the training yard—

Exposed. Caught. //Seen//.

Not as the heir. Not as a warrior learning a new weapon. Not as a prince undergoing a transformation he had not chosen but was enduring with every fibre of a body that fought him at every turn.

As a //spectacle//. A boy with tits. A joke.

The shame was physical. It //sat// in the chest — in the breasts, specifically, which suddenly felt enormous, which suddenly felt like they were a hundred times the small, tender buds they actually were, which suddenly felt like they were the //only thing// my body consisted of, two grotesque swellings on a frame that was neither one thing nor the other, and every eye in the kingdom was fixed on them and laughing.

I wanted to cross my arms over my chest. I wanted to fold forward, curl inward, compress the shameful flesh into invisibility. Every instinct I possessed was screaming: //hide, cover, disappear, they can see you, they can see what you are becoming, they can see the breasts, they know.//

For one terrible moment I was thirteen years old again, naked in the bathing hall, the only boy whose body did not look like the others', too soft, too smooth, and now — decades and magic later — too //female//, and the laughter was the same, the shame was the same, and I could not move.

[[Aldric's response|warrior_queen_day_3_11]]


:: warrior_queen_day_3_11

Aldric's head turned toward the rampart.

Slowly. With the precise, terrible, mechanical deliberation of a siege engine acquiring its target. Every degree of rotation was a promise. Every second of silence was a threat.

The two soldiers on the rampart — I could see them now, young men, barely out of training, their grins dying on their faces as they realised what they had attracted — went perfectly, absolutely, catastrophically still.

<<speech "aldric">>RAMPART!<</speech>>

The word cracked across the Iron Garden like a trebuchet stone striking a city wall. Birds fled from the rampart. The mist shuddered.

<<speech "aldric">>IF YOU HAVE BREATH FOR LAUGHTER, YOU HAVE BREATH FOR THIRTY LAPS IN FULL PLATE! NOW! MOVE!<</speech>>

The two soldiers went white. Not pale — //white//. The colour drained from their faces with a speed that suggested their blood had decided, independently and unanimously, to flee the scene before the rest of them caught up.

They fled. Their boots rang on the stone stairs — panicked, stumbling, the boots of men who had just discovered that mocking the future sovereign in Sir Aldric Vornhelm's training yard was a mistake you made precisely //once// in your life, because there would not be a second opportunity, because after thirty laps in full plate you would be too broken to laugh at anything ever again.

Within moments, I could hear the clanking of hastily donned armour and the desperate, gasping panting of men who were learning — at considerable physical cost — that Sir Aldric Vornhelm protected what was his.

And I was his. His student. His project. His responsibility.

The yard was silent.

Aldric turned back to me. His scarred face was stone. His expression said nothing.

His eyes said everything.

Not pity — never pity. Aldric did not possess pity the way other men did not possess gills; it was simply not part of his design. But something else lived behind those grey eyes in that moment. Something fierce. Something //guarded//. Something that watched the shame on my face and recognised it — not from experience, perhaps, but from the deep, instinctive understanding of a warrior who knew what it was to be vulnerable and refused to let that vulnerability be exploited.

Protectiveness. That was the word. I would only recognise it later, lying in bed with the corset removed and the breasts aching and the shame still smouldering in my chest. But it was protectiveness.

He raised his practice rapier to prima guard. And waited.

[[Resume the drill|warrior_queen_day_3_12]]


:: warrior_queen_day_3_12

I swallowed the burning coal of shame lodged in my throat. It went down hard. It tasted of humiliation and copper and the particular bitterness of being laughed at by people you are supposed to lead.

I raised my blade.

And something... //shifted//. Not in the body — in the //mind//.

The shame was still there. It would be there for hours, perhaps days, a hot coal banked beneath the surface, ready to flare at any reminder. But layered beneath it, like bedrock beneath topsoil, was something else.

//Anger//.

Not the wild, impotent fury of Day 1 — the rage at a body that would not obey. This was colder. Quieter. More //focused//. The anger of a person who has been laughed at and has decided, with a calm that borders on dangerous, that the laughter will stop. Not through authority. Not through punishment. Through //competence//.

They laughed because I was a spectacle. Because the bouncing breasts and the changing body and the prince-becoming-queen was //strange//, and strangeness invited mockery from small minds.

They would stop laughing when the strangeness became //strength//.

I lunged. The rapier's point struck the target dead centre. The sound — a clean //thwack// of blunted steel on straw — was the most satisfying noise I had ever produced.

I recovered. Lunged again. Centre. Again. Centre.

We drilled until the shadows stretched long across the cobblestones. Until my wrist ached from fingertip to elbow. Until the two soldiers on the rampart had long since finished their thirty laps and crawled away to contemplate the wreckage of their choices.

Aldric did not mention the snickering. He did not mention the bouncing. He did not discuss my breasts or my corset or the loosened lacing or the way my body had betrayed me in full view of men I would one day command.

He treated me exactly as he always had: as a student who was not yet adequate, in a body that was not yet finished, and both of those were simply facts to be addressed through repetition and sweat and the stubborn accumulation of skill.

I was grateful beyond the capacity of words to express.

[[After training — the corridor|warrior_queen_day_3_13]]


:: warrior_queen_day_3_13

The walk back to my chambers that evening was different.

The rapier rode at my left hip. Not the longsword. The rapier. A weapon so light I barely felt its presence against my leg — a whisper of weight where the longsword had been a shout. But it was //there//. And its presence at my hip said something that no amount of words could say:

//I am armed. I am training. I am becoming something you have not seen before.//

The corridor was not empty. The evening shift was moving — servants carrying linens, soldiers changing watch, the quiet choreography of a castle at dusk. And they //looked//. As they always looked. Eyes tracking the heir, cataloguing the changes, noting the differences.

But tonight, they saw something new.

Not just the hair. Not just the softened face. Not just the hints of curve beneath the leathers that the corset could smooth but not entirely erase.

They saw the rapier.

A weapon at the heir's hip. A blade. Steel. The visible declaration that whatever the body was becoming, it was becoming something //armed//.

I walked through the corridor with my chin up, my shoulders back — narrower shoulders, yes, but //straight// — and the braid swinging against my back. The gait had the hip-sway that I could not entirely prevent, but the corset controlled it, refined it, and the overall effect was less "boy struggling with new hips" and more... something else. Something that the watching servants and soldiers had not quite seen before.

Maera was waiting at my chamber door. She took one look at me — the rapier at my hip, the sweat on my brow, the expression on my face that was equal parts exhaustion and determination — and tilted her head.

<<speech "maera">>New weapon?<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Rapier. It is lighter. It fits the body better. Aldric says fast kills slow.<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>And the face? That expression that suggests you are planning to either conquer a kingdom or murder someone specific?<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Two soldiers laughed at me. At the... the bouncing. During drills. The corset lacing slipped and the... I was —<</speech>>

I stopped. The shame crept back, warm and unwelcome.

<<speech "maera">>They saw the breasts bounce,<</speech>> Maera said, without inflection. A statement. Not a question. She knew.

<<speech "mc">>Yes.<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>And they laughed.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Yes.<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>And now you are planning to become so devastatingly good with that needle of a sword that no one will ever think about your bouncing breasts again because they will be too busy being terrified of the blade.<</speech>>

I looked at her. She looked at me. Her dark eyes were steady and warm and knowing.

<<speech "mc">>...Yes. Something like that.<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>Good.<</speech>> She opened the chamber door. <<speech "maera">>Come in. Bath is drawn. The corset lacing needs reinforcing — I will fix it tonight. And there is fresh bread from Halda, who says you are too thin and that she will not have a queen who looks as though a stiff breeze could snap her in two.<</speech>>

I followed her into the chamber. The door closed behind me. The rapier hung at my hip, and the corset held my breasts, and the shame was still there but so was the anger, and the anger was //useful//.

[[Evening — the body inspection|warrior_queen_day_3_14]]


:: warrior_queen_day_3_14

Maera fixed the corset lacing while I bathed. She sat on a stool beside the tub with a needle, leather cord, and the training corset across her lap, reinforcing each eyelet with a backstitch that would, she promised, resist a hurricane.

I soaked in the hot water and let the day drain away.

The bath was becoming a ritual. Not the utilitarian scrub of the prince, who had treated bathing as a necessary inconvenience between training sessions. This was something longer. More //attentive//. The hot water found the muscles and unknotted them — shoulders, back, core, thighs, all the infrastructure that Aldric's training was rebuilding from the ground up. And the water found the breasts, and the breasts received it with their particular brand of grateful, aching relief.

They floated. The buoyancy of the water lifted the tissue — small though it was — free of gravity's pull, and the sensation was //rest//. Pure, physical rest for flesh that had been compressed, bounced, compressed again, and generally subjected to more mechanical stress in three days than most breast tissue experienced in a month.

I watched them from above. Two small mounds breaking the waterline. The nipples — softened by the warm water, relaxed from their perpetual state of alertness — just barely visible above the surface. The water lapped at the aerolae, warm tongues of gentle pressure, and each lap produced a subtle, pleasant shiver that I was learning not to flinch from.

<<speech "maera">>The lacing is reinforced. Triple-stitched at every eyelet. If this slips during training, it will be because Aldric has physically torn the garment off your body, and at that point you will have larger concerns.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Thank you.<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>The cups may need adjusting soon.<</speech>> She held the corset up, examining the interior. <<speech "maera">>The breast tissue is pressing against the upper edge of the left cup. There is a millimetre of spillage — the flesh is sitting above the cup's rim rather than inside it.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>I noticed this morning. They are growing faster than Veletia planned for.<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>Nyssa's fifteen percent, yes?<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Apparently my body is //enthusiastic// about growing breasts.<</speech>>

Maera snorted. <<speech "maera">>Your body has always been enthusiastic about everything. You broke three training dummies before you were fifteen. You ate your weight in harvest-feast pie at seventeen. And now you are growing breasts at a rate that has Thalara's apprentice practically vibrating with academic excitement. Consistency is your gift, Caelan.<</speech>>

She set the corset aside and leaned forward, elbows on knees, studying me with her direct, dark gaze.

<<speech "maera">>How does it feel? The rapier, I mean. Not the...<</speech>> she gestured generally at my chest.

<<speech "mc">>It feels... //right//. The longsword was fighting me. The rapier is... talking to me. Responding. The wrists are better for it — thinner, more flexible. The hips drive the rotation. The stance — the feminine guard — the rapier is //designed// for it. Like they were made for each other.<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>They were. The Velmorian court rapier was the Queen's Blades' weapon, three hundred years ago. Women fighting with weapons made for women's bodies. Before the male tradition swallowed it all and decided that if women wanted to fight, they could fight like men or not at all.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Aldric told me that. Almost the same words.<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>He remembers things that matter.<</speech>> She paused. <<speech "maera">>Caelan. The soldiers who laughed today.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>I do not wish to —<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>Listen. The soldiers who laughed today will not be the last. Every woman who has ever trained among men has been laughed at for the way her body moves during combat. Every woman with breasts who has ever run, jumped, climbed, or fought has dealt with the bouncing. It is not shame. It is physics. And the answer is not to bind yourself so tightly you cannot breathe, or to stop moving, or to hide. The answer is to get so good with that blade that the next man who laughs finds the point of it at his throat before the smirk reaches his eyes.<</speech>>

She stood. Gathered the corset, the needle, the thread.

<<speech "maera">>Salve. Both breasts. Circular motions. And then //sleep//. You have that look that says tomorrow will be worse than today, and today was already fairly horrible.<</speech>>

She departed with the corset and a backward glance that said, in the universal language of women who cared too much about people who made it difficult: //You are going to be fine. I am going to make sure of it.//.

[[The salve and sleep|warrior_queen_day_3_15]]


:: warrior_queen_day_3_15

I applied the salve. Both breasts. Circular motions.

My hands had changed further. I noticed it as I smoothed the cream across my own chest — the fingers were longer, definitively, the knuckles less prominent, the nails growing into slightly different shapes — more oval, less square. The palms were narrower. The skin softer. When my fingertips crossed the areola and reached the nipple, the sensation was transmitted through fingers that were becoming instruments of finer resolution — more sensitive themselves, the nerve endings closer to the surface through thinner skin.

I was touching my breasts with hands that were becoming a woman's hands. And the breasts were becoming a woman's breasts. And the //entire system// — hands to breasts, touch to sensation, body to self — was slowly, incrementally, relentlessly //feminizing//.

The nipple pulsed beneath my fingertip. That deep, radiating wave — outward from the nub, through the areola, into the breast, down through the chest. The salve tingled. The moonpetal magic sank in.

I lingered. Not deliberately — not consciously. But my fingertip remained on the nipple for a second longer than the clinical application required, and in that second the sensation shifted from //medical// to something else. Something warmer. Something that lived in the territory between the body's functional responses and its //pleasure// responses, a borderland I had never visited from this direction before.

I pulled my hand away. My breath had quickened — I noticed it with a start, the way the chest rose faster, the way the breasts moved with the accelerated breathing, the way the body was responding to its own touch with a specificity that the prince's flat chest had never possessed.

I wiped the salve from my fingers. Pulled on the sleeping shift. Lay down in bed on my side — the side-sleeping position that accommodated the breasts, left settling against right beneath the binding of gravity.

The rapier leaned against the bedside table. Its slender blade caught the last of the candlelight and gleamed — a thin line of silver in the dark.

Tomorrow, more drills. More lunges. More of the body's rhythms learned and integrated. More of the //fit// between blade and body deepened.

The breasts settled. The wrists ached. The hips were sore from the rotation drills. The core burned with the beautiful, structural pain of muscles being rebuilt.

And the anger from the rampart smouldered beneath it all — not destructive, not consuming, but //warm//. The kind of ember that you could build a fire on. The kind of fire that forged weapons.

I closed my eyes. The rapier gleamed. The body breathed.

Tomorrow, I would be better.

And the day after that.

And every day after that, until //better// became //lethal//, and the laughter stopped, and the only sound that followed me through the corridors of Velmoria was the quiet whisper of a blade at my hip and the silence of men who had learned not to mock what they could not match.

The rapier's weight was nothing against my thigh. Light as a whisper.

But it felt like a promise.

